  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nSadly, the long term forecast looks to be hot and dry. The ever \npersistent upper ridging will be well established over the region by \nSaturday. The upper ridging will continue to expand east across the \nGulf states while multiple upper short waves move on shore over the \nPacific Northwest. Despite the push from these upper shortwaves, the \nupper ridging will be stubborn and remain parked across southeastern \nCONUS. Increasing heights and thicknesses below the ridging will \nkeep temperatures hot. Forecast highs everyday range from the upper \n90s to just below Heat Advisory temperatures (105-109). NBM is \nnotorious for underestimating very hot temperatures for our region. \nThis forecaster would not be surprised if we saw Heat Advisory \ntemperatures sometime next week, especially off the Caprock. There \nis no precipitation in sight as subsidence below the ridging will \nkeep conditions dry. A lee surface trough developing multiple days \nnext week could bring moderate to breezy winds each afternoon. Hot \ntemperatures, dry conditions, and breezy afternoon winds will bring \nthe potential for elevated fire conditions next week.",
